#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3d1a48d-b303-5108-bf3d-41cf2b142017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369170} {:answer/id #uuid "3e591d12-74c2-5577-b196-484985280fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369171} {:answer/id #uuid "320ddc7c-7c7d-541e-9ebe-9ac46df72c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369172} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8f7c53-d3cc-5bfe-9343-72ec3b72a943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369173} {:answer/id #uuid "542a575b-c995-5c99-8b0f-3f774e748289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d1a48d-b303-5108-bf3d-41cf2b142017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369170} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d173091-98b1-5f9e-ba14-223fe159f379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfde08b6-0b33-56dc-ab2e-672049a30b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79811" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0.5,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0.5,1) & =2\\cdot 0.5\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0.5+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-0.5=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+1\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.5.$"}]}
